  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 AM EDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nToday-Tonight...High pressure at the surface slides southward and a \nlittle closer to the area today. This will continue to weaken the \npressure gradient with onshore winds not as breezy today. However, \nnortheast winds will still increase to 10-15 mph in the afternoon, \nwith gusts up to 20-25 mph still possible. A mid/upper level low \nmoves in from the east today, aiding in lift, and combining with an \nincrease in moisture (PW values 1.8-1.9) to help increase rain \nchances today. Scattered to locally numerous showers and storms are \nforecast to develop today, with PoPs ranging from 20-30% NW of I-4 \nto 40-60% across the remainder of east central FL. \n\nOnshore steering winds will continue to generate isolated to \nscattered onshore moving showers and storms along the coast through \nearly this morning. The unsettled pattern should then lead to earlier \ndevelopment of showers and storms along a diffuse inland moving east \ncoast sea breeze from late morning into the afternoon. Cold temps \naloft (around -9 to -10C at 500mb) associated with the closed low \nand lingering pockets of dry air above the surface will increase the \npotential for a few strong storms this afternoon and into the \nevening. Main storm threats will be strong wind gusts to 40-50 mph, \nfrequent lightning, small hail and locally heavy rainfall of 1-3 \ninches that may lead to minor flooding. Onshore moving isolated to \nscattered showers and storms are then forecast to continue into \ntonight.\n\nHighs will continue to be near normal, ranging from the mid to upper \n80s along the coast to upper 80s/around 90 degrees inland. Lows \ntonight will be in the 70s. \n\nSaturday-Sunday...Mid/upper level low aloft will continue to slowly \nprogress westward through the weekend moving into the eastern Gulf \non Sunday. This feature and deeper moisture (PW 1.9-2.0\") will \ncontinue to keep the weather pattern unsettled with near to above \nnormal rain chances across the area. A continued low level onshore \nflow will keep the potential for isolated to scattered onshore \nmoving showers/storms along the coast each morning. Scattered to \nnumerous showers and storms are then forecast to develop with the \ninland moving sea breeze through the late morning and afternoon each \nday. Some stronger storms will continue to be possible each \nafternoon and evening, with frequent lightning, strong wind gusts, \nand locally heavy rainfall of 1-3\" continuing to be the main \nthreats. Temperatures remain near seasonable values, with highs in \nthe mid/upper 80s at the coast and upper 80s/low 90s inland. \nOvernight lows continue in the 70s.  \n\nMonday-Thursday...Low aloft over the eastern Gulf gradually weakens \ninto next week and lifts northeast as it is absorbed by a trough \ntraversing eastward from the central to eastern United States. \nPlenty of moisture will remain across the area to keep shower and \nstorms chances near to above normal into next week, especially as a \nfront is forecast to push southward into the southeast U.S. by the \nmiddle of next week. This will continue daily scattered to numerous \nshowers and storms across the area each afternoon/evening with the \nthreat of a few strong storms continuing. Temperatures may warm ever \nso slightly into next week, but highs still look to remain close to \nnormal.",
